//? Title: SRGAN and ESRGAN: Frozen Features and an Explicit Two-Phase Schedule
//? Subtitle: Native x2 residual generators, live relativistic means and captured BN updates
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Two compact RGB super-resolution models predict a residual over a bicubic image
shortcut. SRGAN uses BN/PReLU blocks and CRD pixel shuffle; ESRGAN uses three
dense residual blocks without BN and relativistic-average adversarial losses.
Both use the actual frozen VGG19 first convolution for content features. The
complete native C++ program keeps pixel-only warm-up, cold Adam reset, fresh
critic-then-generator captures and same-capture BN commits visible. Worked
examples check feature preprocessing/scaling, shuffle order, moving moments and
cross-pool derivatives before fitting. Three fixed epochs reproduce the original
arrays and PNGs exactly. Mean test PSNR improves only.0435/.0277 dB over bicubic;
the report preserves that small result without a perceptual-quality claim.
)
\toc

> Predict an image residual while keeping the shortcut and losses explicit

[Ledig et al., Photo-Realistic Single Image Super-Resolution Using a Generative
Adversarial Network (CVPR2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.04802) combines
content and adversarial training. [Wang et al., ESRGAN: Enhanced Super-Resolution
Generative Adversarial Networks (ECCV2018
workshop)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.00219) uses dense residual blocks without
BN, relativistic discrimination and pre-activation features. These source
experiments are fixed-width4, x2 CIFAR teaching adaptations with a bicubic IMAGE
shortcut and a small normal(.001) output kernel. They are not pretrained SR
benchmark models or full paper architectures.

SRGAN has1591 learned G values in25 leaves,1673 D values in8, plus40 BN saved
values in10 leaves. ESRGAN has3511/40 G and1672/7 D, without BN. The1792 frozen
VGG19 block1_conv1 values belong to neither optimizer. Input geometry is16x16
RGB LR and32x32 RGB HR. Raw generated pixels are unbounded; clipping belongs
only to stated display/metric policies. No generator weight EMA exists.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/asr_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(44, 1500);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle a;
  a.width     = 7.4;
  a.height    = 4.8;
  a.font_size = 40;
  d.node("lr", {-16, 8}, "LR RGB image\n16x16", a)
      .node("g", {-5, 8}, "G residual map\nBN mode explicit", a)
      .node("res", {6, 8}, "Learned residual\n32x32 RGB", a)
      .node("resize", {-16, -8}, "Native bicubic x2\nLR only", a)
      .node("bic", {-5, -8}, "Bicubic shortcut\n32x32 RGB", a)
      .node("add", {6, -8}, "Add image residual\nNo clipping", a)
      .node("image", {17, -8}, "SR image\n32x32 RGB", a)
      .edge("lr", "g")
      .edge("g", "res")
      .edge("lr", "resize")
      .edge("resize", "bic")
      .edge("bic", "add")
      .edge("res", "add")
      .edge("add", "image")
