> Check frozen VGG preprocessing and the exact feature-loss scale

The native extractor reverses RGB to BGR, multiplies by255 and subtracts
(103.939,116.779,123.68), preserving the source Float32 rounding of those means.
It applies the actual frozen3x3x3x64 kernel and64 biases without activation.
The report loads the complete official32-array VGG19 convolution archive and
selects its first convolution; a recorded extractor output or random substitute
is not used. Kernel and bias paths always stop, while generated RGB paths stay
live. The extracted target feature stops before activation/scaling.

SRGAN applies ReLU to each feature tensor, divides BOTH tensors by255, then
computes MSE. The division therefore scales that MSE by255 squared; it is not a
single division of the final MSE by255. ESRGAN divides both pre-activation
features by255, then computes mean L1. The following supplied two-coordinate
fixture makes the distinction explicit, without pretending to be pretrained data.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n    = dm::nn;
  auto t         = n::DType::Float64;
  auto bgr       = n::models::VggImageNetInput(n::tensor({1., 0., .5}, {1, 1, 1, 3}, t));
  auto predicted = n::tensor({-2., 4.}, {2}, t), truth = n::tensor({-1., 2.}, {2}, t);
  auto sr  = n::mean(n::square(n::relu(predicted) / 255. - n::relu(truth) / 255.));
  auto esr = n::mean(n::abs(predicted / 255. - truth / 255.));
  if (std::abs(sr.item() - 2. / 65025) > 1e-15 || std::abs(esr.item() - 1.5 / 255.) > 1e-15)
    throw std::runtime_error("feature loss scaling");
  std::print(
      "| Supplied fixture | Native value |\n|---|---|\n| RGB(1,0,.5) to source-rounded BGR input | "
      "{} |\n| post-ReLU feature MSE after both /255 | {} |\n| pre-activation feature mean L1 "
      "after both /255 | {} |\n",
      bgr.values_str(),
      sr.item(),
      esr.item());
}
```
