> Inspect every saved BN mean update and the distinct two-path predictions

The SRGAN trace below uses all48 after-G saved states, including warm-up. Each
curve is the L2 norm of one layer's four-channel moving mean; it is a summary
of that vector, not a plot of all variance coordinates. The independent audit
checks every mean/variance write bit exactly. Recomputing moments after the first
weight update changes captured moments by up to.00269955397, so the explicit
commit uses the original capture. No optimizer owns those40 saved values.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/asr_bn_history.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace n = dm::nn;
namespace m = n::models;
using A     = std::map<std::string, n::Tensor>;
A Read(const std::string& path) {
  A a;
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz(path))
    a.emplace(key, value);
  return a;
}
int main() {
  const std::vector<std::string> names{
      "residual0/bn1", "residual0/bn2", "residual1/bn1", "residual1/bn2", "body_bn"};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> value(5);
  double                           high = 0;
  for (int e = 1; e <= 3; ++e)
    for (int b = 0; b < 16; ++b) {
      auto a = Read(
          "build/srgan_esrgan/srgan/phases/epoch" + std::to_string(e) + "-batch" +
          std::to_string(b) + "-g-after.npz");
      for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        double v =
            n::sqrt(
                n::sum(
                    n::square(n::cast(a.at("2/" + names[i] + "/moving_mean"), n::DType::Float64))))
                .item();
        value[i].push_back(v);
        high = std::max(high, v);
      }
    }
  auto                           p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  auto                           x = n::arange(1, 49, 1, n::DType::Float64).to_vector<double>();
  std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{
      {37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {22, 163, 74}, {147, 51, 234}, {217, 119, 6}};
  std::vector<std::string> labels{"res0 bn1", "res0 bn2", "res1 bn1", "res1 bn2", "body bn"};
  p.y_limits(-.01, high * 2 + .03);
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
    p.lines(x, value[i], labels[i], colors[i], 2);
  p.x_label("Cumulative G write1..48; saved BN state does not reset at17")
      .y_label("L2 norm of each four-channel moving-mean vector")
      .title("All48 explicit saved-moment commits, separate from Adam")
      .save_svg("build/asr_bn_history.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/asr_bn_history.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Five48-point norm traces summarize all saved moving-mean commits. Variance coordinates are checked in the independent state audit; this figure does not plot them.)
\alttext(Five moving-mean norm curves retain each of48 generator commits across warm-up and GAN phases.)

Read-only validation computes two SRGAN generator paths on the same held-out
LR bank: the deployment prediction reads saved moving statistics, while
prediction_training normalizes using that entire held-out batch. Both remain
immutable and target-independent, but their pixels can differ. ESRGAN has no
BN, so both paths are bit identical. The next figure retains the maximum
within-image path difference for all32 test rows and both models.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/asr_two_paths.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace n = dm::nn;
namespace m = n::models;
using A     = std::map<std::string, n::Tensor>;
A Read(const std::string& path) {
  A a;
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz(path))
    a.emplace(key, value);
  return a;
}
int main() {
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> values;
  double                           high = 0;
  for (const std::string name : {"srgan", "esrgan"}) {
    auto a = Read("build/srgan_esrgan/" + name + "/outputs.npz");
    auto v =
        n::max(
            n::reshape(n::abs(a.at("prediction") - a.at("prediction_training")), {32, 3072}), {1}) *
        1000.;
    values.push_back(v.to_vector<double>());
    high = std::max(high, n::max(v).item());
    if (name == "esrgan" && n::max(v).item() != 0)
      throw std::runtime_error("no BN two-path identity");
  }
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  auto x = n::arange(0, 32, 1, n::DType::Float64).to_vector<double>();
  p.y_limits(-.05, high * 1.8 + .1)
      .lines(x, values[0], "SRGAN paths", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .lines(x, values[1], "ESRGAN paths", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .x_label("All32 held-out test rows in original order")
      .y_label("Max saved-versus-batch pixel difference, multiplied by1000")
      .title("Deployment pixels and batch-objective pixels are separate source fields")
      .save_svg("build/asr_two_paths.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/asr_two_paths.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All64 row maxima are retained. Saved-statistic single/batch prediction agrees with the original source control; the live batch path has a different purpose.)
\alttext(Two32-value curves compare saved-statistic and training-batch pixels, with nonzero SRGAN differences and an exactly zero ESRGAN curve.)

The independent NumPy audit checks362343 native parameter partials and captured
moments(max8.06e-6),10013647 forward values(max1.88e-6),226 scalar finite
differences, all160 Adam transitions bit exactly and every unselected-store/BN
write. It reproduces1327104 HR/LR/bicubic pixels exactly and all768 fitting
row visits. The original1792 feature values match the complete official VGG
archive. All validation/final-test scalar records, target canaries, unequal-pool
relativistic algebra, reset counters and per-image PSNR/SSIM calculations pass.
Original19 archives contain3075843 exact values;515 phase/probe archives add
9487714 exact values. Both PNGs, four TSVs and non-timing metrics match exactly.

These fixed-final results provide only small improvements over bicubic under
the stated luminance/crop metrics. They do not establish sharper perceptual
textures, generalization beyond32 test images or paper-level performance.
Canonical models and frozen-feature/relativistic operations live in the C++ API;
Python/R expose the same owning fields and explicit captures. Full independent
language fitting loops remain a separate migration task. Optional development
oracle: scripts/check_adversarial_sr.py. Dataset preparation and all report
numerical/graphics work use native datamunge APIs.
